window.MANUAL_INDEX = [{"url": "getting-started.html", "title": "Getting started", "summary": "Signing in, the dashboard and the parts of Cupcake.", "text": "Cupcake keeps a laboratory's protocols, experiment sessions, instruments, reagent storage and instrument jobs, with the sample metadata of each job. Signing in Sign in with your username and password, or with ORCID when the site allows it. New users create an account when the site allows registration; otherwise an administrator creates it. Signing in The dashboard After signing in, Cupcake opens on the dashboard: your recent sessions and messages, a running timer and your unread notifications. The home dashboard The navigation bar leads to: Home : projects, lab groups, messages, notifications, your profile and device tokens. Protocols : protocols, sessions and timers. Instruments : instruments, their bookings and maintenance. Storage : storage locations and the reagents in them. Jobs : instrument jobs and their sample metadata. Billing and Admin , for staff. An administrator can hide parts the site does not use."}, {"url": "projects.html", "title": "Projects", "summary": "Grouping jobs and sessions into projects.", "text": "A project groups the instrument jobs and sessions of one piece of work. My Projects lists your projects; selecting one shows its information, jobs and sessions. Projects Create Project creates one with a name and an optional description, and Edit Project changes them. A job's project can also be created while submitting the job; see Instrument jobs ."}, {"url": "protocols.html", "title": "Protocols", "summary": "Writing protocols with sections, steps and reagents, and importing them from protocols.io.", "text": "Protocols lists your protocols, those shared with you and public ones. Selecting one shows its description, number of steps, ratings of complexity and duration, and where it came from. Your protocols, with Create Protocol at the top Creating a protocol Choose Create Protocol , then Create New and enter the title and an optional description. Enable for public view lets others view and use it. Import from protocols.io imports a protocol from its protocols.io address instead, with its steps, sections and reagents. Naming and describing a new protocol The editor opens. The protocol editor, ready for sections and steps Add a section and give it a title and a duration. Adding a section and giving it a title and duration Select the section and Add Step : describe the step and optionally give its duration. Writing a step of the section Save Protocol . The protocol with its section and step Editing a protocol's sections and steps Reagents in steps A step can list the reagents it needs, with a quantity and unit. A scalable reagent's quantity is multiplied by its scaling factor. In the step's description, %ID.name% , %ID.quantity% , %ID.scaled_quantity% and %ID.unit% are replaced by the reagent's name, quantity, scaled quantity and unit, where ID is the step reagent's id; type a space and % in the description to insert them. From a protocol Start Session starts a session from the protocol; see Sessions . Export to protocols.io copies it to protocols.io; see protocols.io ."}, {"url": "sessions.html", "title": "Sessions", "summary": "Carrying out protocols step by step, with notes, recordings, bookings and timers.", "text": "A session is one run of one or more protocols. Everyone in the session sees the same steps, notes and timers. Starting a session On a protocol, choose Start Session , name the session and choose Create & Start Session . A public session can be viewed by everyone. Starting a session from the protocol Sessions lists your sessions; Open Session opens one. The new session in your sessions, with Open Session beside it Sessions Working through the steps The session shows the protocol's sections and the current step, with its instructions and the reagents it needs. Previous Step and Next Step move between steps, in a single view or as a list. The session, showing the first step to carry out Working through a session step by step Annotations Add Annotation adds to the step: Text : a note. Upload : an image, video, audio recording or file. Record : an audio, video or screen recording; audio and video can be transcribed automatically. Sketch : a drawing. Book : a booking of an instrument for the step, showing the instrument's other bookings. Calc and Molarity : a calculator and a molarity calculator, kept with their history. Writing a note on the step The note saved on the step for everyone in the session Book beside a reagent reserves a quantity of it from storage. A booking can be deleted until the site's deletion window before it starts. Timers A step with a duration has one timer, shared by everyone in the session: starting, pausing or resetting it does so for everyone. A step timer running in a session Who can take part Edit on a session sets its name, its planned dates and its editors and viewers : editors can change the session, viewers can only view it. Live Session opens a video call with the others in the session. Exporting Export HTML saves the session with its protocols and annotations as a web page. Export to protocols.io posts it as protocols.io runs; see protocols.io ."}, {"url": "timers.html", "title": "Timers", "summary": "Timers on their own, and the timers of session steps.", "text": "Timers lists your timers: those of session steps and standalone ones. Selecting one shows its duration, status and the history of when it was started, paused and reset, and for a session timer, its session and step. Timers New Timer creates a standalone timer with a name and a duration in seconds. Start , Pause and Reset control it, and Delete Timer removes it."}, {"url": "protocols-io.html", "title": "protocols.io", "summary": "Connecting a protocols.io account, importing protocols and exporting protocols and sessions.", "text": "Connecting your account On your profile, Connected Accounts leads to protocols.io Account : paste an access token from the protocols.io developer page. Replace token changes it and Disconnect removes it from Cupcake. Importing Import from protocols.io imports a protocol from its address or DOI. With an account connected, Browse my protocols.io lists the protocols in your workspaces to import from. Exporting a protocol Export to protocols.io on a protocol copies its title, description, materials, steps and step times onto a protocol on protocols.io. Create an empty protocol there, in the workspace it belongs in, then choose it or paste its address: its content is replaced. The scaling factor multiplies the quantities of scalable reagents. Exporting a session Export to protocols.io on a session posts each of its protocols as a protocols.io run, with the steps' annotations as comments on the matching steps. A protocol whose steps were varied in the session is exported as a new protocol first. Comments show as Anonymous on protocols.io, and uploaded files can be opened by anyone with their address."}, {"url": "instruments.html", "title": "Instruments", "summary": "Instruments, their bookings, maintenance, documents and permissions.", "text": "Instruments lists the instruments you can see; selecting one shows its details. An instrument An instrument's details Overview : its description, whether it accepts bookings, and its pre-approval limits. Metadata : SDRF fields describing it, such as its model, used in the metadata of jobs run on it. Support Information : serial number, vendor, manufacturer, location, maintenance frequency and warranty, and how many days before maintenance or warranty end to notify. Maintenance Logs : routine, emergency and other maintenance, with its status and attached documents. A log can be saved as a template for the next one. Bookings : the instrument's bookings, approved or waiting for approval. Documents such as safety data sheets, certificates and manuals are kept with the instrument, and Manage Contacts keeps the people to contact about it. Bookings New Booking books the instrument from a start to an end time; a booking can be marked as maintenance. A booking is approved straight away when it starts within the instrument's Max Days Ahead and lasts no longer than its Max Days Within Usage , and overlaps no other booking. Otherwise it waits for approval. An instrument that does not allow overlapping bookings refuses a booking that overlaps another. Permissions Manage Permissions gives users one of three levels: View : see the instrument and its information. Book : also book it. Manage : also change its settings and maintenance logs, and its permissions. A disabled instrument is visible to staff only."}, {"url": "storage.html", "title": "Storage and reagents", "summary": "Storage locations, the reagents in them, their quantities and who can use them.", "text": "Storage Management shows storage locations as a tree, such as rooms, freezers, racks and boxes, and the reagents kept in each. A storage location Locations Add Storage adds a location, inside the selected one when one is selected. Manage Access chooses the lab groups that can use a location. Reagents Add Reagent adds a reagent to the selected location with its quantity, unit, molecular weight, expiry date and notes. Notify on low stock sends a notification when its quantity falls below the threshold. Each reagent's actions: Add Quantity and Reserve Quantity record what is added and taken; Usage Journal lists every change. Documents keeps its safety data sheets, certificates and other files. Manage Metadata describes it with SDRF fields. Copy Share Link copies a link to it, and its access settings share it with lab groups, users or everyone. Search reagents finds reagents by name, optionally in nested locations and by molecular weight. Scan Barcode finds them with the camera."}, {"url": "jobs.html", "title": "Instrument jobs", "summary": "Submitting samples to be run on an instrument, with their metadata, and following the job.", "text": "An instrument job asks the staff running an instrument to run your samples. Each job has a metadata table describing its samples. Instrument jobs Submitting a job Submit New Job walks through the submission: Job & Project : name the job, and choose a project or create one. Naming the job and the project it belongs to Lab Group & Staff : choose the lab group and the staff who will run the job, and enter the funder and cost centre. Choosing the lab group and the staff who will run the job Samples : enter the number of samples, which is the number of rows in the metadata table. Giving the number of samples Template : choose the metadata table template for the samples. Searching for and choosing the metadata template for the samples Review & Submit : create the metadata table, fill in its columns, and submit. Reviewing the job before submitting it The job's metadata table created, ready to submit The job after it was submitted Until it is submitted, a job is a draft: Continue Editing Draft returns to it. Submitting an instrument job After submitting Staff review the job and fill in the columns only they edit, such as the instrument settings. The submitted job's metadata table showing only the columns staff fill in Instrument Jobs lists jobs by status (draft, submitted, pending, in progress, completed, cancelled) and type. A job's page shows its information, assigned staff, metadata table and timeline, and its annotations: notes, files, recordings and calculations, from you and from staff."}, {"url": "metadata.html", "title": "Sample metadata", "summary": "Metadata tables of jobs, templates, favourites and vocabularies.", "text": "The metadata tables of jobs follow the SDRF format. Their columns come from templates, their values can be suggested from vocabularies and favourites, and tables are imported and exported as SDRF or Excel files. Table Templates are sets of columns that jobs' metadata tables start from. Column Templates define single columns: their SDRF name, type, ontology and default value. Favorites keeps values used often, for yourself, a lab group or everyone. Vocabularies are the term lists that columns suggest values from. Metadata table templates Metadata column templates Vocabularies In a job's metadata table, Import reads an SDRF or Excel file, Export writes one, Validate SDRF checks the table, and Search & Replace replaces values. Columns marked Staff Only are filled in by staff. The Cupcake Vanilla manual describes these tables in detail."}, {"url": "messages.html", "title": "Messages and notifications", "summary": "Message threads and the notifications Cupcake sends.", "text": "Messages Messages lists your message threads; selecting one shows its messages. Type a message and press Ctrl+Enter to send it. Reading a message thread Notifications Notifications lists what Cupcake has told you, such as low stock, maintenance due and booking changes, by type, priority and whether you have read them. Mark All as Read marks them all. Notifications"}, {"url": "lab-groups.html", "title": "Lab groups", "summary": "Lab groups and sub-groups, their members and what members may do.", "text": "A lab group shares protocols, storage, jobs and metadata among its members. Groups can have sub-groups; open a group to see its sub-groups and members. Lab groups Create Lab Group creates a group, and Create Sub-Group one inside the open group. Allow members to invite others and Allow members to process jobs apply to every member. Invite Members invites users; staff can add users directly without an invitation. An invitation is accepted or rejected from its link. Permissions gives each member View , Invite , Manage (change the group's settings and permissions) and Process Jobs (run the group's jobs)."}, {"url": "profile-and-devices.html", "title": "Your profile and device tokens", "summary": "Your name, email address, password and appearance, connected accounts and device tokens.", "text": "Profile User Profile shows your account and changes your name and email address, with your current password, and your password. Connected Accounts connects protocols.io; see protocols.io . Your profile Appearance chooses light, dark or automatic colours, and the theme: Default or E-Ink , for e-ink displays. Device tokens A device token lets a device, such as a lab display, use Cupcake as you without your password. Device tokens New Token creates one with a label, read-only or read and write access, and an optional expiry date. Copy the token when it is shown: it is not shown again. A token can be rotated or deleted."}, {"url": "billing.html", "title": "Billing", "summary": "Billing records, service tiers, billable item types and prices, for staff.", "text": "Records lists billing records by status (pending, approved, billed, paid, disputed, cancelled), cost centre, funder and period. Select records to approve them, or export them as CSV. Service Tiers sets tiers such as standard or premium, with a price multiplier, a discount, and booking limits. Item Types are what is billed, such as instrument time, with a default unit and whether records need approval. Prices sets the price of each item type for each tier: base price, currency, unit, minimum charge, setup fee, bulk discount and the period it applies to. Billing records Service tiers Service prices A quote for a service is worked out from an instrument, a tier, an item type and a quantity, and downloaded."}, {"url": "administration.html", "title": "Administration", "summary": "Site settings, background workers and users, for staff.", "text": "Site settings Site Configuration sets the site's name, logo and colour, registration and ORCID sign-in, the reagent booking deletion window, and the default Whisper model for transcription. UI Feature Visibility shows or hides metadata tables, instruments, sessions, protocols, messages, notifications, storage, live sessions and billing. Site settings Background workers Worker Status shows the background workers, the job each is running, and the queues. Only superusers see it. Background workers Users User Management lists accounts and creates and edits them, with their active, staff and superuser roles. Managing users"}, {"url": "appliance.html", "title": "Running the appliance", "summary": "Storage, backups, Wi-Fi, remote access, plugins and power on a Cupcake appliance.", "text": "Storage Storage Management mounts a USB drive by its label, or an NFS or SMB share, at /mnt/cupcake-data . Backups Backup Management backs up the database, the uploaded files, or both, to the mounted storage or another folder under /mnt/cupcake-data or /opt/cupcake/backups , and lists the backups made. Wi-Fi WiFi Management connects to a network with a password (WPA2 Personal) or an institutional account (WPA2 Enterprise, such as eduroam). Remote access Remote Access shows whether the appliance is on a Tailscale tailnet or a Cloudflare tunnel, and the commands that set them up. Plugins Plugins lists the installed plugins and their state, starts, stops and restarts them, installs one from a .tar.gz file, and uninstalls them. Power Power restarts the backend or the appliance, or shuts it down. After shutting down, wait until its lights stop before unplugging it."}, {"url": "plugins.html", "title": "Plugins", "summary": "Pages and vocabularies added by plugins.", "text": "Plugins add pages and vocabularies to Cupcake. Their pages appear in the navigation once the plugin is installed; their vocabularies wait for approval by staff before columns can use them."}];
